course: dictionary
lesson: 9758
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The meaning you need depends on the words around it, which is its context. The word supplement is a good example. «B12 comes mainly from animal foods, so vegans usually need a B12 supplement, an extra pill.» Here a supplement is a pill that is taken as well as food.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. «The weekend supplement had games and puzzles.» A supplement can also be an extra part of a newspaper or magazine. Read the whole sentence and ask: what fits best here?
explain: Supplement can also be a verb, a doing word. «Jo took a part-time job to supplement her pocket money.» Here supplement means to add to something to make it bigger or enough. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «to add to her pocket money» still make sense? Then it is right.
fact: A supplement is something added to something else to make it complete or better.
fact: A vitamin supplement is a pill or drink with vitamins, taken as well as food.
fact: A supplement can be an extra part of a newspaper or magazine, or an extra amount of money you pay, such as for a better seat.
fact: In maths, supplementary angles add up to 180 degrees.
fact: Never take a supplement without asking an adult first. «Taking lots of supplements does not usually help healthy children and can sometimes cause harm.»
exercise: «B12 comes mainly from animal foods, so vegans usually need a B12 supplement.» In this sentence, what is a supplement? | A kind of sports shoe | A tall building | *A pill or drink with vitamins, taken as well as food | A pencil case
exercise: In maths, two supplementary angles add up to how many degrees? | 45 | 360 | 90 | *180
exercise: «The weekend supplement had games and puzzles.» What is the supplement in this sentence? | A bag for school books | A kind of tennis ball | A pill for the stomach | *An extra part of a newspaper
test: «The train company charged a supplement for the window seat.» What is the supplement here? | A seat that goes back | A train ticket for children | *An extra amount of money to pay | A broken window
test: «Jo took a part-time job to supplement her pocket money.» What does supplement mean here? | To take away | To forget | *To add to something to make it bigger or enough | To hide
exam: «B12 comes mainly from animal foods, so vegans usually need a B12 supplement.» Why do vegans usually need a B12 supplement? | Because it makes them run faster | *Because B12 comes mainly from animal foods, so food alone may not be enough | Because it tastes sweet | Because animal foods are banned
